11:28:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Your blood is actually never blue—it's always some shade of red, even inside your veins. This is a common misconception, and it comes down to a couple of optical effects: **Why veins look blue through your skin** 1. **Light absorption and scattering**: Skin and tissue absorb and scatter light differently depending on wavelength. Red light penetrates deeper into tissue, while blue light doesn't penetrate as far and scatters back more readily. When light hits a vein beneath the skin, the way these wavelengths interact with skin and tissue makes the vein appear blue or green to our eyes, even though the blood itself is red. 2. **Skin tone and thickness**: The color you perceive is also influenced by the depth of the vein and the thickness/pigmentation of the skin above it. **Why blood doesn't "turn red" from air exposure** Blood is red because of hemoglobin, the oxygen-carrying protein in red blood cells. Hemoglobin bound to oxygen (oxyhemoglobin) is bright red, while hemoglobin that has released its oxygen (deoxyhemoglobin) is a darker, duller red—sometimes described as maroon or brick-red. It's *never* actually blue. So when blood is drawn or you get a cut: - Blood in your veins (which is returning to the lungs to pick up oxygen) is darker red because it has less oxygen. - Once exposed to air, it doesn't "turn red"—it may appear to look a bit brighter red because atmospheric oxygen binds to the hemoglobin, converting some deoxyhemoglobin to oxyhemoglobin at the surface. So the key takeaway: your blood was red the whole time—it's an optical illusion caused by how light interacts with skin that makes veins look blue.
