11:29:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That either/or framing is understandable, but there's a third option you're missing: **teaching shorthand isn't the same as lying.** Think about other things you learned in school that were simplified, not false: - The Bohr model of the atom (electrons in neat orbits) isn't literally accurate, but it's not "lying"—it's a simplified model to build intuition before you learn quantum mechanics. - Maps with colored countries don't mean nations are actually tinted that color from space. - "The sun rises in the east" — the sun doesn't move around the Earth, but nobody's being deceptive by saying it that way. Diagrams using blue for deoxygenated blood work the same way: it's a visual code to help teach a real concept (oxygen content differs between arteries/veins) using colors that are easy to tell apart at a glance. Red-on-red would be hard to distinguish on a printed diagram. But here's the part that should settle it more directly: **this isn't something you have to take on faith from me.** You've almost certainly seen actual blood drawn from a vein—at a doctor's office, a blood donation, a finger prick, a nosebleed, whatever. Did it come out blue? No blood bank, hospital, or lab anywhere on Earth has ever collected blue human blood. If venous blood were truly blue, that would be trivially, constantly observable—not some obscure secret requiring a conspiracy across every medical professional and textbook author for generations. So the actual situation is: - The **biology** taught (arteries = oxygen-rich, veins = oxygen-poor) is correct. - The **color-coding** in diagrams (red/blue) is a teaching convention, not a factual claim about blood's literal color. - The **blue appearance through skin** is a real, well-understood optical effect from how different wavelengths of light penetrate and scatter in tissue. - Nobody is lying—they're using a simplified visual shorthand, the same way virtually all early science education does. It's less "everyone