11:31:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why that feels simpler—it matches what you see and gives a clean cause-and-effect story. But "simpler" only works if it actually matches all the evidence, and this explanation runs into a problem that's easy to check yourself. **The direct test:** When you donate blood, or when blood is drawn via an IV line into a sealed bag, that blood goes straight from your vein into a completely sealed, airless bag/tube. It never touches air at any point. And it comes out dark red, not blue. Same with a C-section or surgery, where surgeons are looking directly at blood inside the body, never exposed to air—still red, just a darker/duller red than arterial blood. If "veins are blue, and blood only turns red on contact with air" were true, blood collected in a sealed bag should look blue. It doesn't. Not once, not ever, in the history of medicine. That's the fact this theory can't get around. What's actually simplest, and matches every piece of evidence (bag draws, surgery, bruises, cuts, octopus blood, everything we've talked about) is: - Human blood is always some shade of red (brighter when oxygen-rich, darker when oxygen-poor), never blue, inside or outside the body. - Veins *look* blue through skin because of how light penetrates and scatters in tissue—it's about your eye and the skin acting as a filter, not the blood changing. I know that's less tidy than "it turns red on contact with air," but the blood-bag/surgery example is the kind of thing that would immediately disprove the air-exposure idea if it were true, and it consistently doesn't happen. So it's not really a case of picking the more complicated of two equally-good explanations—it's that the air-oxidation idea doesn't survive a simple real-world check.
