11:37:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily—that's actually a good example of a common reasoning pattern that doesn't hold up: assuming a property of the *whole system* must belong to *one specific part* of it. A quick everyday example: a stained glass window can look deep blue when sunlight passes through it, but that doesn't mean the sun itself is blue, or that the air behind the glass is blue. The color you see is a property of the whole system—light source + glass + your eye—not necessarily a property of any single piece in that chain. Same logic here: "skin + blood + light hitting your eye" produces blue as an output. That doesn't automatically mean the blood specifically must be blue—it could be the skin doing the filtering, the blood doing the filtering, or some mix. You can't tell just from the combined result which part is "contributing" the blue, without actually isolating the pieces. And we can isolate the pieces, easily: take the blood out from under the skin (blood draw, surgery, donation) — it's red. Every time. That's how we know it's the skin's filtering effect, not the blood itself, contributing the blue — not by assumption, but by literally checking each part separately.
